    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral-exposure records for the structurally related sulfonamide diuretic indapamide show measurable systemic exposure after oral dosing in humans (Cmax 98 ng/mL; dose-normalized AUCt 505–690 ng·h/mL), consistent with oral absorption, although no absolute F is reported.",
      "card_ids": ["C138", "C100", "C113", "C135", "C124"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No L3 record provides an absolute oral bioavailability value for the query or affirmatively supports F < 20%; the L3 family is exposure evidence for other molecules and does not overturn the prior high prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C138", "C100", "C113", "C135", "C124"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "oral_auc_cmax_exposure",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C138", "C100", "C113", "C135", "C124", "C119", "C91"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral-bioavailability record for the query molecule itself is supplied.",
    "No matched oral-versus-IV AUC data for the query are available.",
    "First-pass metabolism and systemic clearance of the query are not directly measured.",
    "L3 records report oral AUC/Cmax for other molecules without IV comparators, so they cannot quantify F for the query.",
    "The closest structural L3 context (indapamide) provides exposure but not absolute bioavailability."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. L3 oral AUC/Cmax records are exposure evidence for other molecules, not query-specific F measurements; the most structurally relevant records (indapamide, a sulfonamide diuretic) show measurable human oral exposure, which is supportive but not decisive. No L3 record affirmatively supports F < 20%. Confidence stays moderate because all evidence is indirect."
}

